          "text": "**Observations:**\n- **Dead border.** Row 0 (inputs 0–27) and the outermost columns (for example column 0 and column 27 in most rows) keep weights of about ±0.036 or less. That is the size of the random initialisation for 784 inputs, so those pixels are almost always black.\n- **Used to the edges.** Apart from that thin border, large learned weights (0.2–0.4) cover nearly the whole canvas:\n  - Row 1 (inputs ~32–51) and row 2 carry big weights over columns ~4–23.\n  - Even row 27, the bottom row, has learned weights up to ~0.31 across columns ~4–23 (e.g. input 770).\n  - Row 26 has strong weights at columns ~4–7 and ~22–23.\n- **Side stripe.** A vertical stripe around columns 23–25 has strong, sign-consistent weights from near the top down to about row 24. Across those rows, unit 12 is roughly +0.2 to +0.35 and unit 15 is roughly +0.15 to +0.29, while units 3 and 11 are negative. There are broad horizontal bands of activity in the middle-left region (unit 3, around +0.2 to +0.28).\n- **Units look at broad regions.** The hidden units respond to the extent of the silhouette (top filled or empty, bottom wide, sides filled) rather than to thin stroke-like patterns.\n- **Output layer.**\n  - Unit 1 has positive top-row weights. It pushes up classes 6, 2, 8 and 3 and strongly pushes down classes 4, 5, 7 and 9.\n  - Unit 7 has strongly negative top-row weights, so it fires when the top of the image is empty. It is the main driver of class 7 (+1.44) and suppresses class 5 (−0.93).\n  - Unit 5 responds to bright bottom-left pixels. It favours classes 9 and 8.\n\n**Reasoning:**\n- **MNIST and EMNIST ruled out.** Digits and letters are size-normalised into a 20×20 box centred in the 28×28 frame. Rows 0–3 and 24–27 are then essentially always zero, and their weights would stay at initialisation. Here rows 1–2 and 26–27 carry some of the largest weights in the network, so the objects fill the whole frame.\n- **Fits clothing silhouettes.** The features are coarse shapes rather than strokes. They separate \"is the top of the frame occupied\", \"is the bottom wide\", and \"are the sides filled\". That suits Fashion-MNIST, where:\n  - garments, bags and trousers are rescaled to touch the top and bottom;\n  - shoes sit low in the frame with an empty top.\n- **Output layer agrees with Fashion-MNIST labels.**\n  - \"Empty top\" pushes towards classes 7 (sneaker, the flattest object) and away from tops and dresses.\n  - \"Filled top\" favours classes 6, 2, 8 and 3 (shirt, pullover, bag, dress) and disfavours 5, 7 and 9 (the footwear classes).\n  - Wide-side and bottom features favour 8 and 9 (bag, ankle boot).\n- **KMNIST less likely.** It also fills the frame, but its brush-stroke characters would give more irregular, stroke-like detectors, and the footwear-versus-tops split seen here would not appear.\n\n**Dataset:** Fashion-MNIST (Zalando's 28×28 grayscale clothing-article images).\n\n**Classes:**\n- 0 T-shirt/top\n- 1 Trouser\n- 2 Pullover\n- 3 Dress\n- 4 Coat\n- 5 Sandal\n- 6 Shirt\n- 7 Sneaker\n- 8 Bag\n- 9 Ankle boot\n\n**Confidence:** 72%",
